1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:04,890
Welcome to the Deep Dive, everyone. Today we are looking at the invisible architecture that, uh,

2
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:08,960
allows massive complex systems to communicate.

3
00:00:09,580 --> 00:00:11,980
We're basing this on the PAERA framework.

4
00:00:12,820 --> 00:00:17,020
So okay, let, let's unpack this. Imagine you're building a massive skyscraper, right?

5
00:00:17,340 --> 00:00:18,860
Right. A huge project.

6
00:00:18,920 --> 00:00:19,400
Exactly.

7
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:27,960
But half your engineering crew thinks the word foundation means the concrete slab, and the other half thinks it means, like, the funding grant.

8
00:00:28,020 --> 00:00:30,160
Which is a complete disaster waiting to happen.

9
00:00:30,220 --> 00:00:35,380
Yeah, exactly. And that exact structural disaster is happening in public IT systems right now.

10
00:00:35,440 --> 00:00:42,120
Oh, absolutely. Because right now, when two different government ministries map out their systems, it's just a total disconnect.

11
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:46,520
I mean, one calls a function a service while another calls the exact same thing a function.

12
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:48,340
Or one says application, the other says system.

13
00:00:48,520 --> 00:00:54,620
Exactly. So without a shared vocabulary, you really can't compare architectures or, uh, spot duplicated work.

14
00:00:54,980 --> 00:00:58,720
You'd never know you're building a registration module that the ministry right next door just finished.

15
00:00:58,900 --> 00:01:00,580
Wow, that's incredibly inefficient. Yeah.

16
00:01:00,840 --> 00:01:03,760
So how does PAERA actually force these blueprints to align?

17
00:01:04,080 --> 00:01:05,900
Well, it uses what we call a meta model.

18
00:01:06,140 --> 00:01:14,860
Mm-hmm. And, uh, rather than thinking of it as just a dictionary that defines words, you should think of it more like a highly standardized Lego set.

19
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:16,440
A Lego set. Okay, I'm listening.

20
00:01:16,780 --> 00:01:25,860
Yeah. So PAERA explicitly restricts you to six core types of blocks or entities, you know, that you're allowed to use on an architecture map.

21
00:01:25,980 --> 00:01:32,000
Right. So if I'm mapping out the Ministry of Education, I can't just draw a cloud and label it tech stuff.

22
00:01:32,060 --> 00:01:33,620
What are my actual constraints here?

23
00:01:33,740 --> 00:01:39,550
You only get those six pieces. Hmm. So, um, if you want to run a national exam, that is your capability.

24
00:01:39,620 --> 00:01:43,500
Okay, and what about the online portal the student uses to access that exam?

25
00:01:43,620 --> 00:01:49,350
That would be the service. The framework restricts applications strictly to the software executing that capability.

26
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:53,620
Oh, okay. Which divorces it entirely from the underlying technology component.

27
00:01:53,660 --> 00:01:58,340
So the technology component is the infrastructure, like your servers or, uh, cloud hosting.

28
00:01:58,670 --> 00:02:03,320
Precisely. Then you add in the data domain, which is the categorized information with a single owner.

29
00:02:03,580 --> 00:02:07,400
Right. And finally, the organization itself, and that covers your entire board.

30
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:11,510
I have to ask though, I mean, doesn't this rigid structure stifle innovation?

31
00:02:12,160 --> 00:02:21,920
If I'm leading an enterprise architecture team, isn't forcing everyone to use the exact same six Lego blocks severely limiting what my developers can actually build?

32
00:02:21,960 --> 00:02:28,460
Well, what's fascinating here is that you adopt this structure from PAERA Annex two from day one.

33
00:02:28,920 --> 00:02:31,500
You aren't wasting time designing the meta model itself.

34
00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:32,340
Oh, I see.

35
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:35,680
Yeah. You aren't boxed in creatively regarding the software you write.

36
00:02:36,020 --> 00:02:38,620
You're just agreeing on the architectural nouns and verbs.

37
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:47,630
And if a specific country genuinely needs an entity that PAERA lacks, you can deliberately extend the framework, provided it's written down and documented.

38
00:02:47,630 --> 00:02:48,370
Okay, that makes sense.

39
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:51,780
But the real power isn't just the fixed entities.

40
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:53,280
It's actually how they connect.

41
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:57,820
Wait, w-- if we're standardizing the boxes- Yeah... wouldn't we have to standardize how they connect?

42
00:02:58,020 --> 00:03:04,980
Yeah. Otherwise, a developer could draw a map where an application somehow owns an organization which, you know, breaks the whole logical hierarchy.

43
00:03:05,040 --> 00:03:08,780
Exactly, yes. The relationships are just as fixed as the entities.

44
00:03:08,930 --> 00:03:12,120
So a capability is always delivered by a service.

45
00:03:12,180 --> 00:03:14,450
A capability is supported by an application.

46
00:03:14,950 --> 00:03:17,340
An application accesses a data domain.

47
00:03:18,240 --> 00:03:20,240
Everything runs on a technology component.

48
00:03:20,760 --> 00:03:26,320
So you literally cannot snap the pieces together in a way that violates that physical constraint.

49
00:03:26,640 --> 00:03:27,340
You really can't.

50
00:03:27,740 --> 00:03:34,740
Which brings us to why this matters for you, the listener, trying to make your own complex systems efficient.

51
00:03:34,820 --> 00:03:40,310
Everyone talks about reuse, you know, building one central identity system for the whole government instead of five separate ones.

52
00:03:40,820 --> 00:03:43,070
But reuse isn't just a matter of good intentions, is it?

53
00:03:43,580 --> 00:03:46,300
No, not at all. It's a structural requirement.

54
00:03:46,360 --> 00:03:55,800
Because before an agriculture ministry can consume a digital identity building block from a central authority, both entities have to describe that block using the

55
00:03:55,860 --> 00:03:57,580
exact same constraints.

56
00:03:57,820 --> 00:04:07,190
So if the agriculture ministry maps its systems in its own bespoke language, integrating that external identity service becomes physically impossible without massive

57
00:04:07,360 --> 00:04:08,670
costly translation layer.

58
00:04:08,760 --> 00:04:10,680
Exactly. It becomes a huge mess.

59
00:04:10,720 --> 00:04:18,040
And this shared language completely changes how abstract policy translates into concrete IT architecture.

60
00:04:18,079 --> 00:04:20,620
Like, let's say a head of policy comes in with a directive.

61
00:04:21,120 --> 00:04:23,670
They say, "We want to register every learner once."

62
00:04:23,800 --> 00:04:24,670
Right. A common goal.

63
00:04:24,980 --> 00:04:28,300
Yeah. In the old chaotic method, IT hears that

64
00:04:28,940 --> 00:04:36,340
and might build a sprawling, redundant mess of databases and user interfaces because the word register means something different to everyone.

65
00:04:36,500 --> 00:04:42,800
Right. But with the PAERA meta model, business and IT are finally holding the exact same map.

66
00:04:43,060 --> 00:04:48,210
IT hears register every learner once, and they instantly translate that policy into the framework.

67
00:04:48,400 --> 00:04:54,690
So they'd say, "Okay, that is exactly one capability exposed via one service governed by one data domain."

68
00:04:55,100 --> 00:04:57,660
Spot on. The meta model acts as the ultimate bridge.

69
00:04:57,980 --> 00:05:04,460
It really is the invisible architecture that turns cross-departmental reuse from a wishful thought into an actual executable plan.

